1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,992
A team has fifty ideas for making delivery better.

2
00:00:03,992 --> 00:00:04,970
Faster builds.

3
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:05,668
New tools.

4
00:00:05,668 --> 00:00:06,785
More automation.

5
00:00:06,785 --> 00:00:07,903
A new dashboard.

6
00:00:08,453 --> 00:00:10,223
They start all fifty at once.

7
00:00:10,223 --> 00:00:11,261
Six months later,

8
00:00:11,261 --> 00:00:12,481
nothing is finished,

9
00:00:12,481 --> 00:00:14,618
and nobody can say what got better.

10
00:00:15,168 --> 00:00:17,574
A good improvement plan is the opposite.

11
00:00:17,574 --> 00:00:18,295
It is small.

12
00:00:18,295 --> 00:00:19,738
It starts with a number,

13
00:00:19,738 --> 00:00:20,761
picks one change,

14
00:00:20,761 --> 00:00:22,144
and ends with a number.

15
00:00:22,694 --> 00:00:27,131
Today, we build that plan for CloudCart, and we check it in a real terminal.

16
00:00:28,681 --> 00:00:30,110
Here is what to expect.

17
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:33,647
One: why plans fail, and what a good plan looks like.

18
00:00:34,197 --> 00:00:36,629
Two: the six parts of an improvement plan.

19
00:00:37,179 --> 00:00:41,787
Three: a worked plan for CloudCart, using the numbers from the last two lessons.

20
00:00:42,337 --> 00:00:46,881
Four: a live demo. We check a weak plan, then a good one, then you write your own.

21
00:00:47,431 --> 00:00:50,823
This is lesson 8 of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

22
00:00:52,873 --> 00:00:55,070
First, why do improvement plans fail?

23
00:00:55,620 --> 00:01:00,762
Reason one: they are vague. Be faster. Improve quality. Nobody can tell if it worked.

24
00:01:01,312 --> 00:01:03,746
Reason two: they change too much at once.

25
00:01:03,746 --> 00:01:05,053
If five things change,

26
00:01:05,053 --> 00:01:07,072
you cannot learn which one helped.

27
00:01:07,622 --> 00:01:09,545
Reason three: they have no number.

28
00:01:09,545 --> 00:01:11,016
Without a starting number,

29
00:01:11,016 --> 00:01:12,430
you cannot show progress.

30
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:13,392
Without a target,

31
00:01:13,392 --> 00:01:15,259
you never know when you are done.

32
00:01:15,809 --> 00:01:18,068
And reason four: they ignore people.

33
00:01:18,068 --> 00:01:20,201
A tool does not improve a process.

34
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:22,209
People who agree on the goal do.

35
00:01:22,759 --> 00:01:25,810
A good plan fixes all four. Let us see its parts.

36
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,323
A practical plan has six parts.

37
00:01:29,873 --> 00:01:30,974
One: the baseline.

38
00:01:30,974 --> 00:01:32,136
Where are we today?

39
00:01:32,136 --> 00:01:33,176
Use real numbers,

40
00:01:33,176 --> 00:01:35,867
like the four metrics from lesson 150.

41
00:01:36,417 --> 00:01:37,624
Two: the bottleneck.

42
00:01:37,624 --> 00:01:39,434
Which one step is the slowest?

43
00:01:39,434 --> 00:01:42,028
Lesson 151 showed how to find it.

44
00:01:42,578 --> 00:01:46,994
Three: one change. Exactly one. Something that attacks that bottleneck.

45
00:01:47,544 --> 00:01:48,645
Four: the target.

46
00:01:48,645 --> 00:01:49,940
A number and a date.

47
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:50,718
For example,

48
00:01:50,718 --> 00:01:52,855
lead time from three days to two,

49
00:01:52,855 --> 00:01:54,733
by the fifteenth of December.

50
00:01:55,283 --> 00:01:58,419
Five: how you will measure. What do you check, and how often.

51
00:01:58,969 --> 00:01:59,834
Six: the risk.

52
00:01:59,834 --> 00:02:01,068
What could go wrong,

53
00:02:01,068 --> 00:02:02,611
and how will you recover?

54
00:02:02,611 --> 00:02:04,093
Every change has a risk,

55
00:02:04,093 --> 00:02:06,500
and a plan that says so is more honest.

56
00:02:08,050 --> 00:02:10,887
Now, CloudCart. Our example online shop.

57
00:02:11,437 --> 00:02:13,346
Today it deploys once a week.

58
00:02:13,346 --> 00:02:16,704
A change takes about three days to reach customers.

59
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:18,942
Seventeen percent of deploys fail.

60
00:02:18,942 --> 00:02:20,653
Restoring takes two hours.

61
00:02:21,203 --> 00:02:24,790
Measuring the stages shows the bottleneck: manual approval.

62
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:26,491
Thirty hours out of seventy,

63
00:02:26,491 --> 00:02:28,862
waiting for a person to click a button.

64
00:02:29,412 --> 00:02:30,966
So the change is simple.

65
00:02:30,966 --> 00:02:35,238
Replace the manual approval with an automatic check: tests passed,

66
00:02:35,238 --> 00:02:35,950
scan clean,

67
00:02:35,950 --> 00:02:36,921
budget healthy.

68
00:02:37,471 --> 00:02:41,802
The target: lead time from three days to two, by the fifteenth of December.

69
00:02:42,352 --> 00:02:45,709
The risk: the automatic check could miss a bad change.

70
00:02:45,709 --> 00:02:47,946
So we keep one-click rollback ready,

71
00:02:47,946 --> 00:02:49,563
from lesson 146.

72
00:02:50,113 --> 00:02:51,766
Notice what we did not do.

73
00:02:51,766 --> 00:02:53,993
We did not rewrite the application.

74
00:02:53,993 --> 00:02:55,392
We did not buy a tool.

75
00:02:55,392 --> 00:02:56,346
One bottleneck,

76
00:02:56,346 --> 00:02:57,046
one change.

77
00:02:58,596 --> 00:03:01,839
What comes after the first change? Your plan needs an order.

78
00:03:02,389 --> 00:03:04,432
Start with changes that are small,

79
00:03:04,432 --> 00:03:05,454
safe and visible.

80
00:03:05,454 --> 00:03:06,957
A quick win builds trust,

81
00:03:06,957 --> 00:03:09,962
and trust is what you need for the bigger changes.

82
00:03:10,512 --> 00:03:13,403
Then repeat the loop from lesson 151.

83
00:03:13,403 --> 00:03:13,895
Measure.

84
00:03:13,895 --> 00:03:15,186
The bottleneck moves.

85
00:03:15,186 --> 00:03:16,293
Pick the next one.

86
00:03:16,843 --> 00:03:19,318
And share the numbers with everybody.

87
00:03:19,318 --> 00:03:20,054
Developers,

88
00:03:20,054 --> 00:03:20,589
testers,

89
00:03:20,589 --> 00:03:21,325
operations,

90
00:03:21,325 --> 00:03:21,927
security.

91
00:03:21,927 --> 00:03:24,335
Shared numbers are shared ownership.

92
00:03:24,335 --> 00:03:26,944
That is the first principle of DevOps,

93
00:03:26,944 --> 00:03:28,683
from lesson 141.

94
00:03:29,233 --> 00:03:32,923
So an improvement plan is not a document that sits in a folder.

95
00:03:32,923 --> 00:03:35,207
It is a loop that a team runs together.

96
00:03:36,757 --> 00:03:40,810
Go to the demo folder.

97
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:44,259
Look around.

98
00:03:44,809 --> 00:03:46,345
Four files and a script.

99
00:03:46,895 --> 00:03:51,268
Today's numbers.

100
00:03:51,818 --> 00:03:53,053
One deploy a week.

101
00:03:53,053 --> 00:03:54,494
Lead time three days.

102
00:03:54,494 --> 00:03:56,346
Seventeen percent failures.

103
00:03:56,346 --> 00:03:57,787
Two hours to restore.

104
00:03:57,787 --> 00:04:00,394
And the slowest step: manual approval.

105
00:04:00,944 --> 00:04:05,318
A weak plan.

106
00:04:05,868 --> 00:04:06,576
Look at it.

107
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:06,898
Slow.

108
00:04:06,898 --> 00:04:07,607
Everything.

109
00:04:07,607 --> 00:04:08,251
Be faster.

110
00:04:08,251 --> 00:04:10,183
And three changes in one line.

111
00:04:10,183 --> 00:04:12,502
This is the kind of plan that fails.

112
00:04:13,052 --> 00:04:18,898
Check the weak plan.

113
00:04:19,448 --> 00:04:24,994
Six fixes needed. Every part is missing or too vague. The script tells you exactly which.

114
00:04:25,544 --> 00:04:29,640
A good plan.

115
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:31,784
A real number for today.

116
00:04:31,784 --> 00:04:32,714
One slow step,

117
00:04:32,714 --> 00:04:33,777
with its number.

118
00:04:33,777 --> 00:04:34,507
One change.

119
00:04:34,507 --> 00:04:35,902
A target with a date.

120
00:04:35,902 --> 00:04:36,566
A measure.

121
00:04:36,566 --> 00:04:37,828
And an honest risk.

122
00:04:38,378 --> 00:04:40,447
Check the good plan.

123
00:04:41,997 --> 00:04:45,581
All six parts are fine. One change, one number, one date.

124
00:04:46,131 --> 00:04:49,992
Write your own.

125
00:05:02,242 --> 00:05:06,061
Check your plan.

126
00:05:07,611 --> 00:05:10,704
Your practice task: write and check an improvement plan.

127
00:05:11,254 --> 00:05:15,158
One: read the baseline, and the weak plan. Find what is wrong with it.

128
00:05:15,708 --> 00:05:18,972
Two: check the weak plan with the script, and count the fixes.

129
00:05:19,522 --> 00:05:21,890
Three: write your own plan, in six lines.

130
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:25,875
Four: run the script on it, until every line says okay.

131
00:05:27,925 --> 00:05:31,146
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

132
00:05:31,696 --> 00:05:37,371
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

133
00:05:37,921 --> 00:05:41,974
Two: the folder academy/lesson152-demo.

134
00:05:42,524 --> 00:05:43,957
Three: a baseline file,

135
00:05:43,957 --> 00:05:44,704
a weak plan,

136
00:05:44,704 --> 00:05:45,452
a good plan,

137
00:05:45,452 --> 00:05:47,009
and a script called plan,

138
00:05:47,009 --> 00:05:47,943
with a read me.

139
00:05:48,493 --> 00:05:50,170
Four: clone the repository,

140
00:05:50,170 --> 00:05:51,413
and copy the folder.

141
00:05:51,413 --> 00:05:52,965
You need a shell and awk,

142
00:05:52,965 --> 00:05:54,829
which every Linux machine has.

143
00:05:55,379 --> 00:05:57,237
Five: no lab in this domain.

144
00:05:57,237 --> 00:05:59,892
The next lesson is the mastery scenario,

145
00:05:59,892 --> 00:06:01,352
and it uses this plan.

146
00:06:03,402 --> 00:06:05,877
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

147
00:06:06,427 --> 00:06:08,560
One: the weak plan needs six fixes.

148
00:06:09,110 --> 00:06:11,713
Two: the good plan has one change, not three.

149
00:06:12,263 --> 00:06:14,588
Three: the target has a number and a date.

150
00:06:15,138 --> 00:06:17,975
Four: your plan starts from a real baseline number.

151
00:06:18,525 --> 00:06:21,512
Five: your plan names a risk, and a way to recover.

152
00:06:22,062 --> 00:06:24,302
Six: your script run says plan ready.

153
00:06:24,852 --> 00:06:27,817
All six, and you can write a practical improvement plan.

154
00:06:29,867 --> 00:06:31,638
Your turn, on your own machine.

155
00:06:32,188 --> 00:06:34,022
One: copy the demo folder.

156
00:06:34,572 --> 00:06:37,495
Two: run the script on the weak plan and the good plan.

157
00:06:38,045 --> 00:06:42,482
Three: write a plan for your own team, or for a project you know. Six lines.

158
00:06:43,032 --> 00:06:46,083
Four: run the script, and fix every line it flags.

159
00:06:46,633 --> 00:06:48,895
Five: show the plan to one colleague.

160
00:06:48,895 --> 00:06:51,585
If they cannot say what changes and by when,

161
00:06:51,585 --> 00:06:52,564
make it smaller.

162
00:06:53,114 --> 00:06:54,948
Pause the video, and begin now.

163
00:07:10,498 --> 00:07:11,373
Quick check.

164
00:07:11,923 --> 00:07:13,672
Name the six parts of a plan.

165
00:07:14,222 --> 00:07:15,609
Why only one change?

166
00:07:16,159 --> 00:07:17,759
What does a good target look like?

167
00:07:18,309 --> 00:07:19,439
Why name the risk?

168
00:07:19,989 --> 00:07:25,387
Objective covered: MC16, objective 8: a practical improvement plan.

169
00:07:25,937 --> 00:07:28,006
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

170
00:07:38,556 --> 00:07:42,183
Next time: the mastery scenario for DevOps fundamentals.

171
00:07:42,733 --> 00:07:46,061
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
